{% # theme-check-disable %}
{%- comment -%}
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  METRICS-MATCHED FALLBACKS (FOUT size-jump fix)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  2026-07-03 ROOT CAUSE UPDATE: the recurring site-wide "text shifts
  size/shape on load" was NOT a FOUT and none of the tuning below could
  ever fix it. Klaviyo's onsite-forms script injects
  <style id="kl-custom-fonts"> importing Google Fonts Inter
  (display=swap) under the SAME family name "Inter" — later-declared
  faces win, and Google's Inter build is ~6% wider than Shopify's, so
  the whole page re-rendered ~2s after paint. Fix: theme fonts are now
  declared under the private family "<family> Casa" (see
  snippets/casa-font-face.liquid) which app-injected fonts cannot
  capture. The fallbacks below remain for genuine slow-network FOUT.
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  When a web font loads after first paint, the system fallback renders
  at one set of glyph metrics and the loaded font renders at a different
  set — even at the same `font-size` in CSS. The visible result is a
  layout/size jump on swap (text appears small, then enlarges when the
  web font kicks in). `font-display: optional` is supposed to prevent
  swaps, but the browser still shows the fallback briefly during the
  100ms block period, and on slow paints the size difference is plainly
  visible (per the screen recording attached to the FOUT report).

  Solution: declare a synthetic `<font> Fallback` family that points at a
  system font (Arial / Helvetica) but applies size-adjust + ascent /
  descent / line-gap overrides chosen so its rendered glyph box matches
  the real font's. Add the synthetic family to the font stack ahead of
  the generic sans-serif. Now when the fallback paints, it occupies the
  same vertical space as the real font — when the real font swaps in,
  nothing reflows. (Recipe values come from Next.js's well-tested font
  fallback table for the corresponding font family.)

  Currently we're only doing this for Inter (the merchant's heading
  font, see config/settings_data.json: type_heading_font = "inter_n7").
  If the merchant switches the heading family in Shopify, this fallback
  becomes a no-op (the font stack still references "Inter Fallback" but
  the metrics no longer match the new family) — at worst we degrade to
  the FOUT we had before this fix. To re-tune for a different family,
  measure empirically: render the same word with the loaded font vs
  Arial-with-different-size-adjust values and pick the value that
  matches widths within 0.5%.

  CALIBRATED VALUES (size-adjust: 113%, ascent-override: 95%): the
  generic Next.js Inter recipe (size-adjust: 107.4%, ascent: 90.2%)
  undershot by ~5.8% on Shopify's CDN-served Inter italic at the hero
  accent's actual font-size and weight. Live measurement of "Light" at
  font-size: 108px / weight 700 / italic showed:
    Inter (loaded):   243.97px wide
    Arial @ 107.4%:   229.92px wide  (-5.76%)  ← was visibly smaller
    Arial @ 113%:     243.04px wide  (-0.38%)  ← chosen
    Arial @ 114%:     245.38px wide  (+0.58%)  ← also acceptable
  113% sits within 1px of the loaded font's width — no perceptible
  size jump on swap.
{%- endcomment -%}
<style>
  /*
    REVERTED 2026-05-12: I tried to "improve" this with a fallback
    chain like `local('-apple-system'), local('BlinkMacSystemFont'),
    local('Segoe UI'), local('Roboto'), local('Arial')` thinking it
    would prefer the system font (SF on iOS) for tighter shape
    match. That broke iOS Safari — `-apple-system` and
    `BlinkMacSystemFont` are CSS font-family KEYWORDS, NOT real
    font names that `local()` accepts. iOS Safari treats the whole
    @font-face as malformed when it hits invalid local() arguments
    and pulls text invisible until a scroll-triggered repaint.

    Going back to the calibrated, working version:
    Arial only. The size-adjust 113% was measured against Arial
    specifically and verified to within 0.4% width match on the
    hero accent.

    If we want true SF-on-iOS shape matching later, the correct
    approach is a UA-conditional fallback (Liquid or
    media-query-prefers-color-scheme-style detection) — NOT
    passing CSS keywords to local().
  */
  /*
    2026-05-12: REMOVED `ascent-override`, `descent-override`,
    `line-gap-override` from both blocks. iOS Safari does not
    support these descriptors (WebKit bug 219735, status: NEW,
    unresolved as of April 2025). They were silently dropped on
    every iPhone — which left iOS with a PARTIAL metric match
    (width via size-adjust 113%, but ascent / descent / line-gap
    using Arial's native values). When real Inter swapped in,
    width stayed the same but the vertical metrics jumped, which
    is exactly what the user reported as "font sizes change
    rapidly on initial load."

    Per multi-agent research consensus (caniuse, WebKit bug
    tracker, Shopify Dawn / Horizon themes, Stripe / Linear /
    Vercel implementations, Capsize docs):

      - `size-adjust` — supported in iOS Safari 17.0+ (Sept 2023).
        KEEP. Matches width across all platforms.
      - `ascent-override` — NOT supported in any iOS Safari.
        REMOVE — causes partial-match jump on iOS.
      - `descent-override` — same. REMOVE.
      - `line-gap-override` — same. REMOVE.

    Without the three unsupported descriptors, Arial uses its
    native vertical metrics on both platforms. Inter's vertical
    metrics are close enough to Arial's that the post-swap shift
    is barely perceptible — far less than the previous "width
    matched but height jumps" partial state.

    DO NOT re-add these descriptors until the WebKit bug is
    resolved AND the merchant's iOS minimum is bumped to that
    Safari version.

    Why we don't self-host Inter as a theme asset: the same agent
    research established that fonts.shopifycdn.com is the same
    edge as cdn.shopify.com, Dawn + Horizon both use Shopify's
    font_face filter, and self-hosting loses Shopify's per-store
    subsetting. Self-hosting is more work for ~zero benefit.
  */
  /*
    2026-05-12 split: one fallback per style (Next.js pattern).
    113% was calibrated for italic-700 at 108px (the hero accent
    word "Light") and matches Inter italic within 0.4%. But Arial
    UPRIGHT vs Inter UPRIGHT has a different aspect ratio than
    their italic counterparts — at 113% the H1 upright lines
    were over-widened, producing a visible narrow-on-swap when
    Inter loaded inside font-display: optional's ~100ms window.

    Upright now uses 107% (Next.js generic Inter recipe, close
    match for Arial-upright vs Inter-upright). Italic stays at
    113% (empirically validated for the accent). Selectors that
    need the italic match must explicitly request
    'Inter Fallback Italic' — see .casa-hero__title-gradient.
  */
  @font-face {
    font-family: 'Inter Fallback';
    src: local('Arial');
    size-adjust: 107%;
  }
  @font-face {
    font-family: 'Inter Fallback Italic';
    src: local('Arial Italic'), local('Arial');
    font-style: italic;
    size-adjust: 113%;
  }
</style>
{%- comment -%}
  All font preloads stay at fetchpriority="high".

  REVERTED 2026-05-11: an earlier attempt downgraded body / subheading /
  accent fonts to fetchpriority="low" on the theory that the LCP image
  was competing for HTTP/2 high-priority slots. That broke iOS Safari:
  the hero title is hidden via opacity:0 until JS adds `.fonts-ready`,
  which is gated by `document.fonts.ready`. That promise waits for ALL
  @font-face fonts to settle — including body / subheading / accent.
  Downgrading those fonts to "low" priority extended the wait window
  on slow iOS connections from ~1 second to ~10 seconds, leaving the
  title invisible for the entire load.

  The merchant's recorded iOS reproduction (10 second invisible-title
  window) traced directly to this regression. Going back to all-high
  preloads costs ~3 high-priority HTTP/2 slots that the LCP image
  could otherwise own, but the alternative is broken title rendering
  on iOS Safari — unacceptable. To free those slots without breaking
  iOS, the proper fix is to change the JS to check ONLY the heading +
  heading-bold-italic fonts (via document.fonts.check()) instead of
  document.fonts.ready (which waits for all). That's a separate change.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- comment -%}
  Preload dedup tracker. When two font slots resolve to the same file
  (e.g. merchant has both `type_heading_font` and `type_accent_font`
  set to Inter weight 700 → both resolve to `inter_n7.woff2`) the
  browser would otherwise see two `<link rel="preload">` for the same
  URL — wasted HTTP/2 high-priority slot, duplicate entry in the
  resource graph, and a Lighthouse "Avoid multiple preloads for the
  same resource" finding. Track URLs as we render and skip dupes.
  Same dedup pattern as theme-styles-variables.liquid's
  `rendered_fonts` tracker for `font_face` calls.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign rendered_preload_urls = '' -%}

{%- unless settings.type_body_font.system? -%}
  {%- assign body_url = settings.type_body_font | font_url -%}
  {%- unless rendered_preload_urls contains body_url -%}
    <link
      rel="preload"
      as="font"
      href="{{ body_url }}"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
      fetchpriority="high"
    >
    {%- assign rendered_preload_urls = rendered_preload_urls | append: body_url | append: '|' -%}
  {%- endunless -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Light (300) variant of the body font — used by .cpm__title on
    PDPs (block: casa-title.liquid) as the visible product title.
    Without preload + real @font-face, the browser synthesizes Light
    from Regular and produces a visible mid-paint swap when Inter
    finally loads (Elie reported 2026-05-14 on Apōllō Floor Lamp).
    Real @font-face for 300 is declared in theme-styles-variables.liquid.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign body_light = settings.type_body_font | font_modify: 'weight', '300' -%}
  {%- if body_light and body_light.system? == false -%}
    {%- assign body_light_url = body_light | font_url -%}
    {%- unless rendered_preload_urls contains body_light_url -%}
      <link
        rel="preload"
        as="font"
        href="{{ body_light_url }}"
        type="font/woff2"
        crossorigin
        fetchpriority="high"
      >
      {%- assign rendered_preload_urls = rendered_preload_urls | append: body_light_url | append: '|' -%}
    {%- endunless -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endunless -%}
{%- unless settings.type_subheading_font.system? -%}
  {%- assign subheading_url = settings.type_subheading_font | font_url -%}
  {%- unless rendered_preload_urls contains subheading_url -%}
    <link
      rel="preload"
      as="font"
      href="{{ subheading_url }}"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
      fetchpriority="high"
    >
    {%- assign rendered_preload_urls = rendered_preload_urls | append: subheading_url | append: '|' -%}
  {%- endunless -%}
{%- endunless -%}
{%- unless settings.type_heading_font.system? -%}
  {%- assign heading_url = settings.type_heading_font | font_url -%}
  {%- unless rendered_preload_urls contains heading_url -%}
    <link
      rel="preload"
      as="font"
      href="{{ heading_url }}"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
      fetchpriority="high"
    >
    {%- assign rendered_preload_urls = rendered_preload_urls | append: heading_url | append: '|' -%}
  {%- endunless -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Hero accent word ("Light" on the homepage) and every section H2
    italic-gradient `<em>` accent (brand book) use heading font at
    weight 700 + italic. Without preloading the bold-italic variant
    the browser briefly renders a lighter-weight italic on first
    paint before swapping (visible FOUT). The `font-display: optional`
    set in theme-styles-variables.liquid makes the swap less common,
    but `optional` still allows the font to apply if it arrives
    within the first paint window — which is exactly when the swap
    was happening here. Preloading the bold-italic variant closes
    that window so the correct weight is on the very first frame.
    Used site-wide (not homepage-only) because every section H2
    carries an italic-gradient `<em>` accent per the brand book.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign heading_bold_italic = settings.type_heading_font | font_modify: 'weight', 'bold' | font_modify: 'style', 'italic' -%}
  {%- if heading_bold_italic and heading_bold_italic.system? == false -%}
    {%- assign hbi_url = heading_bold_italic | font_url -%}
    {%- unless rendered_preload_urls contains hbi_url -%}
      <link
        rel="preload"
        as="font"
        href="{{ hbi_url }}"
        type="font/woff2"
        crossorigin
        fetchpriority="high"
      >
      {%- assign rendered_preload_urls = rendered_preload_urls | append: hbi_url | append: '|' -%}
    {%- endunless -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endunless -%}
{%- unless settings.type_accent_font.system? -%}
  {%- assign accent_url = settings.type_accent_font | font_url -%}
  {%- unless rendered_preload_urls contains accent_url -%}
    <link
      rel="preload"
      as="font"
      href="{{ accent_url }}"
      type="font/woff2"
      crossorigin
      fetchpriority="high"
    >
    {%- assign rendered_preload_urls = rendered_preload_urls | append: accent_url | append: '|' -%}
  {%- endunless -%}
{%- endunless -%}
{% # theme-check-enable %}
